#: ../source/python/plot.rst:10
msgid "Visualisation of meshes and fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:12
msgid ""
"The module ``getfem_plot`` displays meshes and finite element fields "
"directly from |py|, in the same spirit as the functions ``gf_plot_mesh``, "
"``gf_plot`` and ``gf_plot_slice`` of the Matlab/Octave interface. It relies "
"on two optional libraries, which are only imported when they are used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:17
msgid ""
"`Matplotlib <https://matplotlib.org>`_, for 1D and 2D plots (well suited to "
"figures for papers) and small 3D surfaces;"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:19
msgid ""
"`PyVista <https://pyvista.org>`_ (based on VTK), for 3D plots that can be "
"rotated, zoomed and panned with the mouse, also inside Jupyter notebooks, or"
" rendered off-screen to image files."
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:23
msgid "They can be installed with::"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:27
msgid ""
"In 3D, PyVista is used when it is available; otherwise the module falls back"
" on Matplotlib with a warning (Matplotlib is slow for large surfaces and "
"only sorts the faces approximately in depth). The renderer can be chosen "
"explicitly with ``backend=\"matplotlib\"`` or ``backend=\"pyvista\"``."
msgstr ""

