#: ../source/python/plot.rst:143
msgid "Saving figures"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:145
msgid ""
"In the interactive PyVista window, the key ``i`` saves the current view "
"(after any rotation or zoom) in the files ``getfem_plot_001.png``, "
"``getfem_plot_002.png``, ... of the current directory, without overwriting "
"existing files. This is set by the options ``save_key``, ``save_filename`` "
"(possibly with a counter field ``{:03d}``) and ``save_scale`` (magnification"
" of the image with respect to the window)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:154
msgid ""
"The format is given by the extension: bitmap (``png``, ``jpg``, ...) or "
"vector graphics (``svg``, ``eps``, ``ps``, ``pdf``, ``tex``). In vector "
"formats, the colour gradients of the surfaces are less faithful than in a "
"bitmap of high resolution (``save_scale=3`` for instance)."
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:159
msgid ""
"From a script, ``gfp.save_view(pl, \"fig.png\")`` saves the view of a "
"plotter ``pl``. To keep the view chosen interactively, show the window with "
"``pl.show(auto_close=False)``: after closing it, ``gfp.save_view`` saves the"
" last view as a bitmap image, and ``pl.camera_position`` can be stored to "
"reproduce the same view later off-screen. Images can also be produced "
"without any window with ``off_screen=True, screenshot=\"file.png\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../source/python/plot.rst:166
msgid ""
"The Matplotlib windows have their own button to save the figure, and "
"``fig.savefig(\"file.pdf\")`` can be used in scripts."
msgstr ""

